Use `Bundle records` to package project data alongside its metadata and media
for publishing, archiving, or reproducible analysis. Before writing anything,
`Package contents` shows every file, record count, column, and warning.

## Choose a bundle format {% #choose-a-bundle-format %}

| Format | Choose it when | It holds | NAHPU can import it |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **Darwin Core Archive** (`.dwca.zip`) | A museum database or biodiversity portal (such as GBIF) asks for a DwC-A | Specimens as one flat core table, with parts, measurements, and media in extension tables | No |
| **Darwin Core Data Package** (`.dwc-dp.tar.gz`) | The recipient supports the newer relational Darwin Core standard | Specimens, events, identifications, parts, and people in linked Darwin Core tables | No |
| **NAHPU Data Package** (`.nahpu-dp.tar.gz`) | You need everything for R or Python analysis, long-term archiving, or a full project copy | The complete project, every populated NAHPU table, user configs, and files | Yes, via `Import project` or `Merge project` |

Darwin Core bundles include only values with an exact, registered Darwin Core,
Dublin Core, or Audubon Core term. Choose a **NAHPU Data Package** when nothing
may be left out, including custom fields. Any
[Frictionless](https://frictionlessdata.io) tool can read it; see
[Tutorial 3](/en/tutorials/export/custom-data-package-script/).

## Create a bundle

{% steps %}

1. Open the project `Dashboard` menu and select `Bundle records`.
2. Choose a `Bundle format` {% ref id="B" /%}.
3. Choose an `Archive format` {% ref id="C" /%} when the format supports more
   than one container:
   - `TAR.GZ` is the default for Data Packages.
   - `ZIP` is available for tools and operating systems that prefer standard
     ZIP archives.
   - Darwin Core Archive always uses `ZIP`.
4. For a Darwin Core bundle, select `All taxa` {% ref id="A" /%} or
   `Selected taxa`. Selecting Mammals also includes Bats.
5. Enter a `File name` {% ref id="D" /%} (optionally turn on `Append date`) and
   choose a destination folder on desktop.
6. Inspect `Package contents` {% ref id="E" /%} (under the `Contents` tab on
   phones). Expand any CSV resource {% ref id="F" /%} to inspect its columns.
7. Review any warnings, such as missing media or the DwC-DP ZIP compatibility
   notice.
8. Select `Create bundle` {% ref id="G" /%}. You can cancel while the bundle is
   being built. Once finished, share, open the folder, save a copy, or select
   `Bundle another`.

{% /steps %}

{% aside type="caution" title="Review before sharing" %}

NAHPU Data Packages contain complete active-project transfer data and user
configs, which can include personnel details, project locations, media, and
custom fonts. Review them before sharing publicly.

{% /aside %}

## Choose an archive container

| Container | Typical extensions | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `TAR.GZ` | `.dwc-dp.tar.gz`, `.nahpu-dp.tar.gz` | Default for Data Packages and the standards-oriented choice for DwC-DP. Extracts `datapackage.json` and root metadata at the archive root. |
| `ZIP` | `.dwca.zip`, `.dwc-dp.zip`, `.nahpu-dp.zip` | Required for Darwin Core Archive; normal option for NAHPU Data Package; compatibility option for DwC-DP (triggers a warning because DwC-DP guidance prefers gzip). |

## Deep dive

The sections below describe what each bundle writes and how NAHPU maps records
into it. Use them when a recipient asks about package structure or when you
process a package in scripts.

### Darwin Core Archive {% #darwin-core-archive %}

A Darwin Core Archive (DwC-A) is a specimen-focused exchange package following
the [Darwin Core Text Guidelines](https://dwc.tdwg.org/text/).

#### Scope and field coverage

The selected taxon groups determine which specimen occurrences are exported.
NAHPU follows links from those specimens to include related collecting events,
specimen parts and material entities, measurements, media, and people
(collectors, catalogers, preparators, and media creators). Empty optional
fields and empty extension tables are omitted.

A column is written only when it has an exact, registered Darwin Core, Dublin
Core, or Audubon Core term; fields without one are omitted. `Package contents`
lists any withheld fields before export. Use a NAHPU Data Package when every
recorded value must be preserved.

#### Root files and relationships

{% filetree %}
- meta.xml
- eml.xml
- occurrence.csv
- material.csv
- measurement_or_fact.csv
- multimedia.csv
- media/
{% /filetree %}

Only files backed by selected data are written:

- `meta.xml` describes the occurrence core, extension files, column positions,
  and Darwin Core term URIs.
- `eml.xml` holds dataset-level metadata.
- `occurrence.csv` is always the core table.
- Extension rows link back to the occurrence core: specimen parts become
  `material.csv` records, measurements become `measurement_or_fact.csv`
  records, and `multimedia.csv` rows reference both the occurrence and the
  packaged path under `media/`.

Output: `<file-name>.dwca.zip`.

### Darwin Core Data Package

A Darwin Core Data Package (DwC-DP) represents Darwin Core data as normalized,
relational tables under the
[Darwin Core Data Package specification](https://dwc.tdwg.org/dp/) and the
Frictionless Data Package model.

#### Root files

{% filetree %}
- datapackage.json
- eml.xml
- occurrence.csv
- identification.csv
- event.csv
- material.csv
- occurrence-assertion.csv
- media.csv
- agent.csv
- occurrence-agent-role.csv
- event-agent-role.csv
- material-agent-role.csv
- media-agent-role.csv
- occurrence-media.csv
- media/
{% /filetree %}

Optional tables appear only when data exist. `datapackage.json` identifies the
versioned DwC-DP profile and declares every CSV resource, media type, field
order, primary key, foreign key, and relationship predicate. Structural `_pk`
and `_fk` columns preserve relational links alongside human-readable IDs.

#### Data mapping

NAHPU maps specimen data into normalized classes rather than repeating fields
on `occurrence.csv`:

- `occurrence`: the specimen occurrence;
- `identification`: determination and taxonomic ranks;
- `event`: collecting activity and locality context;
- `material`: specimen parts, catalog, and preparation details;
- `occurrence-assertion`: measurements and non-class assertions (such as host
  association);
- `agent` and `*-agent-role`: people and their roles (preferring canonical
  ORCID URLs with NAHPU UUIDs as fallback);
- `media` and `occurrence-media`: media metadata and links.

The same strict term-coverage rule applies as for DwC-A. Original DDM, DMS, or
UTM strings stay in verbatim-coordinate terms, positive capture extent adds to
coordinate uncertainty, and selected weight units are preserved.

For invertebrates, sex, life stage, caste, host association, and specimen
remarks are occurrence terms; host part and optional morphometrics (head width,
body length, upper and lower wingspan) export as MeasurementOrFact assertions
in millimetres. Canopy cover and environmental parameters attach to the
collecting event with defined units (`°C`, `%`, `mg/L`, `m/s`, `mm`, `okta`).

Output: `<file-name>.dwc-dp.tar.gz` (default) or `<file-name>.dwc-dp.zip`
(compatibility option, with a warning).

### NAHPU Data Package {% #nahpu-data-package %}

NAHPU Data Package is NAHPU's complete-data and reproducibility format—a
Frictionless Data Package enriched with NAHPU metadata. Unlike Darwin Core
bundles, it is not filtered by taxon group: it bundles the full active-project
transfer JSON, matching typed CSV tables, enum mappings, controlled-vocabulary
snapshots, user configs, and associated files.

It is the only bundle that preserves custom fields without a Darwin Core term.
Only tables populated by the project are exported (a mammals-only project
carries no bird, herpetofauna, invertebrate, or fish tables), and
`mappings/sqlite_enums.csv` lists only the enum columns of those populated
tables.

#### Root layout

{% filetree %}
- datapackage.json
- nahpu.toml
- nahpu-project.json
- tables/
  - project.csv
  - ...
- configs/
  - user_configs.json
- mappings/
  - sqlite_enums.csv
- vocabularies/
  - site.csv
  - events.csv
  - specimens.csv
  - symbionts.csv
- files/
  - ...
- media/
  - ...
{% /filetree %}

Empty project tables are omitted from `tables/` and `datapackage.json`, while
metadata resources (`mappings/sqlite_enums.csv` and `vocabularies/*.csv`) are
always present—with header rows only if empty.

#### Project JSON and CSV tables

`nahpu-project.json` is the same versioned payload created by `Export project`
(active project, related records, media manifest, metadata, and warnings),
preserving distinctions like `null` versus an empty string without requiring
SQLite. The CSV files under `tables/` are generated from the same payload so
JSON and tables stay aligned; empty collections stay in `nahpu-project.json`
for project transfer even when omitted as CSVs.

#### SQLite enum mappings

`mappings/sqlite_enums.csv` decodes integer enum indexes stored in the exported
tables. Each row records the SQLite table, column, Dart enum or logical type,
zero-based integer index, stable code name, and human-readable NAHPU label.

Included mappings (filtered to the populated tables in the package) cover:

- specimen sex (`mammalAttribute`, `birdAttribute`, `herpAttribute`,
  `invertebrateAttribute`, `fishAttribute`, and `specimenIndividual`), using
  explicit stable codes where legacy codes `0`, `1`, and `2` keep their
  original meanings;
- identification confidence (`specimen.iDConfidence`);
- fish specimen type (`Individual` / `Lot`) and invertebrate caste;
- mammal reproductive fields and echolocation categories;
- avian reproductive and molt fields.

Boolean integers and numeric measurements are not enum indexes and are not
listed.

#### Controlled vocabularies

Four CSV resources snapshot the active controlled vocabularies from NAHPU
Settings (or NAHPU defaults if unchanged):

- `vocabularies/site.csv`: site types and habitat types;
- `vocabularies/events.csv`: collecting methods and collecting personnel roles;
- `vocabularies/specimens.csv`: specimen types, treatments, conditions,
  enabled specimen-sex options, identification methods, and life stages;
- `vocabularies/symbionts.csv`: symbiont interaction types, categories,
  detection methods, preparation methods, anatomical locations, storage, and
  treatments.

Each row records the config key, vocabulary name, zero-based position, and
value, so analysis tools can read vocabularies directly without parsing
`configs/user_configs.json`.

#### Frictionless descriptor and analysis

`datapackage.json` uses the standard `data-package` profile, declaring each
populated table as a UTF-8 `tabular-data-resource` with column order, types,
required constraints, primary keys, and foreign keys, along with schemas for
the enum-mapping and vocabulary CSVs and entries for non-tabular resources.

Extract the archive before loading `datapackage.json`, because paths are
relative to the package root. Check whether an optional table exists before
reading it:

```python
from collections import Counter

from frictionless import Package

package = Package("/path/to/extracted-package/datapackage.json")
print(package.resource_names)

if package.has_resource("specimen"):
    specimens = package.get_resource("specimen").read_rows()
    counts = Counter(row["taxonGroup"] or "Unspecified" for row in specimens)
    print(counts)
```

```r
library(frictionless)

package <- read_package("/path/to/extracted-package/datapackage.json")
resource_names(package)

if ("specimen" %in% resource_names(package)) {
  specimens <- read_resource(package, "specimen")
  print(table(specimens$taxonGroup, useNA = "ifany"))
}
```

#### Reproducibility manifest, configs, and files

- **`nahpu.toml`** records the format name and version, export timestamp,
  application name/version/build number, database and user-config schema
  versions, table/enum/vocabulary counts, and compiled `nahpu_api` crate
  versions (`nahpu_dp`, `nahpu_dwc`, `nahpu_db`, `nahpu_configs`, and others).
- **`configs/user_configs.json`** holds the versioned user-config export
  (general settings, record-export presets, document templates, and print-layout
  presets).
- **`media/` and `files/`** carry referenced project media, referenced
  personnel photos (under `media/` with transfer-compatible paths), and custom
  user fonts (under `files/`). Missing files trigger warnings rather than
  failing silently.

Output: `<file-name>.nahpu-dp.tar.gz` (default) or `<file-name>.nahpu-dp.zip`,
with identical internal contents.

## Bundle versus backup

| | Darwin Core bundle | NAHPU Data Package | Database backup |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Restores a NAHPU project | No | Yes (`Import project` or `Merge project`) | Yes (entire installation) |
| Primary purpose | Exchange with biodiversity systems | Documented, reusable copy of one project | Full device recovery |

A NAHPU Data Package covers one project only, so continue making regular
[database backups](../#backup-database) for full-device recovery.

Contributors extending bundle fields or taxon groups can consult
[Export workflows](../../../contributing/code/data-export-workflows) and
[Adding a taxon group](../../../contributing/code/adding-a-taxon-group).

## Package validation and warnings

Before writing, NAHPU validates required fields and relationships; afterward,
it reopens the archive to confirm root metadata exist. Warnings—such as missing
linked media or DwC-DP ZIP compatibility mode—do not always block export, so
review them before sharing or depositing the bundle.
